[[:db/retract 17592186167314 :question/answer-fields 17592186165093] {:db/id 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-96f65a49-2a61-5981-a404-aa212d4de13c" "question-ac9ae842-1c73-58e9-9ed3-756e4fa56ec1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166506 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/63/630f2ae400ab4ccd212138703f93516384c23b284f5ecac5b8c459964f436d0b.svg)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/ff/fffb7d52ff1ec576d336b3498e40ea433546d971a398550848359cda657f957c.svg)\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n![](images/d5/d5c965f4aa3a43689e92d60295049b1d7388c0d7eaf8b8ee93c1bd7eec4af207.svg)\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/e4/e4b23c56134ac2b5f813ebbe4aa89f4ca4fc35a9c96be1471b4d37915270e46f.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166507 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/11/11d3e9693d6aa5f2b7836f4efad788fc8693d8e28ed2f0d8b5bf3ea2b9937850.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-96f65a49-2a61-5981-a404-aa212d4de13c" :question/id #uuid "96f65a49-2a61-5981-a404-aa212d4de13c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331097" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a22c5a1e-381b-53d5-9e61-1f0e13b8d114"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a22c5a1e-381b-53d5-9e61-1f0e13b8d114" :answer-field/id #uuid "a22c5a1e-381b-53d5-9e61-1f0e13b8d114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5551b66-4909-5792-8d3d-3a30ec491dc1" "answer-b09a08e9-f214-5a5c-b110-f1010c4b03ee" "answer-c068b9da-8858-56ed-90bd-f563b1b66009" "answer-59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" "answer-7ad62917-fe79-54d5-9e2a-eb52a863023e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5551b66-4909-5792-8d3d-3a30ec491dc1" :answer/id #uuid "a5551b66-4909-5792-8d3d-3a30ec491dc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b09a08e9-f214-5a5c-b110-f1010c4b03ee" :answer/id #uuid "b09a08e9-f214-5a5c-b110-f1010c4b03ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c068b9da-8858-56ed-90bd-f563b1b66009" :answer/id #uuid "c068b9da-8858-56ed-90bd-f563b1b66009" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" :answer/id #uuid "59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ad62917-fe79-54d5-9e2a-eb52a863023e" :answer/id #uuid "7ad62917-fe79-54d5-9e2a-eb52a863023e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac9ae842-1c73-58e9-9ed3-756e4fa56ec1" :question/id #uuid "ac9ae842-1c73-58e9-9ed3-756e4fa56ec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331980" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3adf9e22-bc52-5ded-9787-739dfa3cf328"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3adf9e22-bc52-5ded-9787-739dfa3cf328" :answer-field/id #uuid "3adf9e22-bc52-5ded-9787-739dfa3cf328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" "answer-91bf1bed-82dd-5957-bd20-b0c4787407e0" "answer-4677b7a7-7f61-5a88-a5ee-a34ee62c287d" "answer-96da30d2-eb7e-540b-b235-30c2de13031a" "answer-ad2050e5-3099-5de8-9248-cf99c56b8941"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" :answer/id #uuid "c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91bf1bed-82dd-5957-bd20-b0c4787407e0" :answer/id #uuid "91bf1bed-82dd-5957-bd20-b0c4787407e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4677b7a7-7f61-5a88-a5ee-a34ee62c287d" :answer/id #uuid "4677b7a7-7f61-5a88-a5ee-a34ee62c287d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96da30d2-eb7e-540b-b235-30c2de13031a" :answer/id #uuid "96da30d2-eb7e-540b-b235-30c2de13031a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad2050e5-3099-5de8-9248-cf99c56b8941" :answer/id #uuid "ad2050e5-3099-5de8-9248-cf99c56b8941" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167314 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-56cd2b18-0b66-5513-b0b3-8386a57997dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-56cd2b18-0b66-5513-b0b3-8386a57997dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "56cd2b18-0b66-5513-b0b3-8386a57997dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a376-310fe63163d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0abeb303-65a1-580a-8d0c-2e6a1c960b4d" "answer-5d9de9ca-9cf2-557a-8cdb-19ad0d5e57b5" "answer-69db9e77-56bd-5ad8-9ac5-e356be91510d" "answer-f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a376-310fe63163d0" "answer-18398e71-d597-5623-af51-1629bf876027"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0abeb303-65a1-580a-8d0c-2e6a1c960b4d" :answer/id #uuid "0abeb303-65a1-580a-8d0c-2e6a1c960b4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9de9ca-9cf2-557a-8cdb-19ad0d5e57b5" :answer/id #uuid "5d9de9ca-9cf2-557a-8cdb-19ad0d5e57b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69db9e77-56bd-5ad8-9ac5-e356be91510d" :answer/id #uuid "69db9e77-56bd-5ad8-9ac5-e356be91510d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a376-310fe63163d0" :answer/id #uuid "f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a376-310fe63163d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18398e71-d597-5623-af51-1629bf876027" :answer/id #uuid "18398e71-d597-5623-af51-1629bf876027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167979 :tutorial/content "A **decoupled system** of ODEs is a system in which each equation involves only its own dependent variable. For a linear homogeneous system, this means each equation has the form\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nFor example, the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis decoupled, while the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis *not* decoupled.\n\nIn matrix form, a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nis decoupled exactly when $A$ is a diagonal matrix. For example:\n\n- The system\n![](images/d8/d8f25a6f8f1d636e750d597907bc686acb5768f37fbbc60b0e07ee31519e3dea.svg)\nis decoupled since its corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\n- The system\n![](images/17/170c78d470ac7d7af444604f8be7eb850248a36bd2b1a1179e9c4a1c76ab19d4.svg)\nis *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167980 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider the following decoupled system:\n![](images/30/3059139f7e3fc43137dcf6422cb3e2e73649e643968e980ab3fa5c2a60062172.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is diagonal, we can solve the two differential equations separately using elementary calculus:\n\n- The solution to $x_1'(t)={3}x_1(t)$ is $x_1(t)=c_1e^{{3}t}$ for $c_1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- The solution to $x_2'(t)={-5}x_2(t)$ is $x_2(t)=c_2e^{{-5}t}$ for $c_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the solution of the system as\n![](images/03/03e6bf38a720a04bbfb14b7eb712693bce86838d2981a0df6fe66fefef5c5952.svg)\n\nIn general, for a decoupled system\n![](images/b9/b95e096702bebeaaae7e6b2ef9762960a4dbe8ffb5b9a46e05cdd40e1a42d484.svg)\nthe formula for the **general solution** is\n![](images/a3/a3b7aad0b63afbe8e087cb488bc0ba29a7cfaab5c5ca3f4f0bd8fe57fe15121a.svg)\n\nNote that the set \n![](images/ef/efcb42f86c9c1b64f7db619d82c8eebad19fd5f3e05a946a3d34963c4de9f334.svg)\nalways forms a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions to the system." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167981 :tutorial/content "Consider the following decoupled system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the *general solution* to this is given by\n\n![](images/79/79d3209f0112e97df3ece546181add70427484455248e907993fa9a617b60bd0.svg)\n\nBut sometimes we may need to find a **particular solution** where we give specific values to the constants $c_1$ and $c_2.$\n\nFor example, suppose that $x_1$ and $x_2$ represent the number of birds of species $A$ and $B,$ respectively, that inhabit a particular island, and the above system models the growth rates of the two species. \n\nGiven this information, we might want to know how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years. To answer this question, we need an **initial condition**, which is some extra information about the system at a particular moment in time. \n\nSuppose that at $t=0,$ there were initially $20$ birds of species $A$ and $2\\,000$ birds of species $B.$ We can write this initial condition as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e83003ab2d4d19328189f5cff0d3542a48af224e17cf2d5de0d6cddc5971548.svg)\n\nThen, by substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we can find specific values of $c_1$ and $c_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 2\\,000\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $c_1 = 20$ and $c_2 = 2\\,000$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is given by \n\n![](images/f1/f101144c98365f20dce4ce0fb6e41cc2f8cf55b64cc19a77fa63da6a4a48415e.svg)\n\nFinally, we can find how many birds of each type there will be after $t=3$ years by substituting $t=3$ into the particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =20[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3}+2\\,000[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20{e}^{3} \\\\ 2\\,000{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}401.7 \\\\ 99.6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that there will be approximately $402$ birds of species $A$ and $100$ birds of species $B$ after $t=3$ years." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167009 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
